---
title: Scales
description: Start with compact scales, upgrade individual mappings to D3 when their semantics require it, and keep responsive range ownership clear.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts accepts callable, copyable scale factories and instances. Start\nwith the exact compact scale entry that matches the mapping. Upgrade only the\naxis, color, or radius mapping whose semantics require D3."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack compact scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" cover numeric linear, categorical band and point,\nand ordinal mappings without a production D3 dependency."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"D3"}]},{"type":"text","value":" adds temporal, nonlinear, radial, interpolated, and statistical scale\nsemantics plus optional shape, time, and spatial algorithms."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" infers factory domains from mark channels, assigns\nresponsive pixel ranges, lays out guides, compiles scenes, and renders them."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compact and D3 scales implement the same chart-facing contract and can be used\nin one definition. There is no hidden D3 umbrella import."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts"},{"type":"text","value":" declares "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-array"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-geo"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-delaunay"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-hexbin"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-contour"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-force"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-sankey"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-hierarchy"},{"type":"text","value":" because its\ntransforms, polar and D3 curve features, geo features, and optional spatial,\nnetwork, and hierarchy entries own those implementations. They are not peers\nand require no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"use"},{"type":"text","value":" configuration. Bundlers tree-shake unused algorithms and\ngeometry, and exact feature subpaths remain available when an application\nwants a narrower import."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"start-with-compact-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with compact scales"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install TanStack Charts for ordinary numeric and categorical charts:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm add @tanstack/charts","lang":"sh"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import one exact family:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is intentionally no aggregate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/scales"},{"type":"text","value":" export. Each exact scale subpath\nfits the same callable, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"domain"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"range"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"copy"},{"type":"text","value":" contract consumed by\nTanStack Charts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The compact linear scale has numeric, two-stop domains and ranges. It supports\nmapping, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clamp"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice"},{"type":"text","value":", ticks, basic numeric tick formatting, and\ncopying. The categorical families support D3-compatible domain interning,\npadding, alignment, rounding, bandwidth, unknown values, and copying."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the smallest family that preserves the data's meaning:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upgrade when"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric x or y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/linear"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mapping needs piecewise domains, nonnumeric output, or custom interpolation"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Categories with width, such as bars"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/band"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mapping needs behavior outside the documented compact band contract"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Categories without width, such as line or dot positions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/point"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The values must instead be spaced by elapsed time"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable categorical colors"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The color mapping is sequential, diverging, quantile, quantize, or threshold"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dates spaced by elapsed time and calendar-aware ticks"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleUtc"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"—"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logarithmic, power, symlog, square-root, or radial mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The corresponding "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" family"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"—"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleBand"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scalePoint"},{"type":"text","value":" accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"},{"type":"text","value":" values as categories. They preserve\ndistinct dates and first-seen order, but they do not represent elapsed time. A\nFriday and the following Monday occupy adjacent categorical positions. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleUtc"},{"type":"text","value":" when the weekend must occupy its real temporal span or\nwhen the axis needs calendar-aware ticks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An axis formatter does not by itself require a larger scale. Pass\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Intl.NumberFormat"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Intl.DateTimeFormat"},{"type":"text","value":", or another application formatter to\nthe guide. Upgrade to D3 when the scale's own tick, interpolation, or domain\nsemantics are required."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"upgrade-one-scale-at-a-time","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upgrade one scale at a time"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A definition does not need one scale implementation for every mapping. This\ntime-series chart upgrades x to D3 while keeping its ordinary numeric y scale\ncompact:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@types/d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" because this source imports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleUtc"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe compact scale entries ship their own declarations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"direct-dependency-ownership","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct dependency ownership"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If application source imports a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-*"},{"type":"text","value":" module, declare that module and its\nmatching TypeScript package directly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm add d3-scale\npnpm add -D @types/d3-scale","lang":"sh"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not declare a D3 module merely because another package uses it internally. A\nchart that directly upgrades only its temporal axis should declare only\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":"; bundlers should not retain unused shape, force, geo, zoom, or\nhierarchy code. Apply the same direct-dependency rule when source imports\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-array"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"},{"type":"text","value":", or another granular D3 module."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This rule also applies when definitions live in framework component source.\nThe adapter mounts a definition; it does not own the D3 imports used to author\nit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts"},{"type":"text","value":" declares "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-array"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-geo"},{"type":"text","value":" because its\nnumeric-bin and stack transforms, polar and D3 curve features, and geo features\nown those implementations. They are not peers and require no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"use"},{"type":"text","value":"\nconfiguration. Bundlers tree-shake unused algorithms and geometry, and exact\nfeature subpaths remain available when an application wants a narrower import."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"capability-map","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capability map"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the official D3 pages as the API reference for each algorithm. TanStack Charts documentation only describes how its output crosses the chart boundary."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Need"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"D3 module"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How it enters TanStack Charts"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quantitative, temporal, categorical, log, radial, and color scales"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-scale","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a factory for an inferred domain or an instance for a fixed domain"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequential, diverging, and categorical color schemes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-scale-chromatic","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale-chromatic"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass an interpolator or scheme to a configured D3 color scale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extents, grouping, aggregation, bins, sorting, and statistics"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-array","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-array"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Convert source data into rows, domains, or thresholds before creating marks"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stacks, pies, arcs, curves, and shape generators"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-shape","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feed pie intervals and curve factories to polar marks, or bridge a Cartesian curve with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3Curve"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calendar intervals"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-time","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-time"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build bins, ticks, rounded selections, and date windows in application code"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric formatting"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-format","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-format"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a formatter to an axis or tooltip option"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Time formatting"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-time-format","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-time-format"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a formatter to an axis or tooltip option"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quadtrees"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-quadtree","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-quadtree"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implement an optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpatialIndexFactory"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Delaunay and Voronoi geometry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-delaunay","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-delaunay"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use exact optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delaunayLink"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"voronoi"},{"type":"text","value":" marks; import directly for a custom spatial index or geometry"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DOM selection for optional D3 gesture controllers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-selection","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-selection"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Imported privately by first-party brush and zoom behaviors; import directly for a different DOM controller"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Brushes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-brush","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-brush"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use exact optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"brushX"},{"type":"text","value":"; import directly for a different application-owned gesture"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pan and zoom"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-zoom","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-zoom"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use exact optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoomX"},{"type":"text","value":" with a controlled semantic window; import directly for a different gesture policy"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hierarchies and layouts"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-hierarchy","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-hierarchy"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use exact optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"treeLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" for flat tidy trees and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"treemap"},{"type":"text","value":" for responsive rectangle tiling"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Force simulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-force","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-force"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use exact optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forceLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" for static settlement; import directly for a live application controller"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sankey flow layout"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/d3/d3-sankey","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-sankey"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use exact optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sankeyDiagram"},{"type":"text","value":" for responsive layout and ordinary child-mark composition"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Geographic projections and paths"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://d3js.org/d3-geo","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-geo"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a responsive projection factory to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An optional algorithm does not necessarily need final chart layout.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"treeLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forceLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" produce semantic data-space coordinates, so\nnative "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"link"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":" marks can map their output through ordinary\npositional scales. Treemap topology depends on the final plot aspect ratio and\nits padding is measured in pixels, so the exact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"treemap"},{"type":"text","value":" mark owns both the\nresponsive D3 layout and its downward-increasing screen coordinates. Sankey\ncolumn allocation, padding, and proportional link width also resolve in final\npixels; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sankeyDiagram"},{"type":"text","value":" then composes ordinary marks over immutable node and\nlink rows. Density estimation and Delaunay geometry likewise run after scale\nranges resolve. Keep live force controllers in application state; the exact\nCharts force entry owns deterministic static settlement only."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"positional-scales-follow-materialized-dimensions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Positional scales follow materialized dimensions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every materialized positional dimension declares its scale:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst spec = {\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleBand },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Positionless marks use explicit null entries:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, frame } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst borderOnlyChart = defineChart({\n  marks: [frame()],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mark with x values requires a non-null x scale. A mark with y values requires\na non-null y scale. One-dimensional charts use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" only for the unused\nentry. The scale factory chooses the mapping; materialized mark channels\nsupply its domain."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"factory-domains-come-from-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Factory domains come from marks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the factory itself when the domain should cover the rendered data:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'month', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scalePoint },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Continuous factories use the finite extent of their channels. Band and point\nfactories use distinct values in first-seen order. Bars and areas include zero\nwhen they use an implicit zero baseline. Empty channels retain the factory's\nnative domain."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return a scale from a zero-argument factory when it needs configuration before\ndomain inference:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.16),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the axis "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nice"},{"type":"text","value":" option because nicening must happen after inference."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"fixed-domains-remain-application-semantics","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed domains remain application semantics"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a scale instance when the domain must not follow the rendered marks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst normalizedY = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]),\n}\n\nconst windowedX = {\n  scale: scaleUtc().domain([windowStart, windowEnd]),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Be equally deliberate with:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether a log scale is valid for all values"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether time is local or UTC"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which categories exist when some are filtered out"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether multiple facets share a domain"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether a color domain must remain stable across sessions"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The instance rule is the same for compact and D3 scales. Use the implementation\nthat owns the required mapping semantics, then configure the application-owned\ndomain on that instance."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"responsive-ranges-belong-to-tanstack-charts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive ranges belong to TanStack Charts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not assign pixel ranges to positional scales used by the chart:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const xScale = scaleUtc\nconst yScale = scaleLinear","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For each scene, TanStack Charts:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creates a factory scale or copies a configured instance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calculates the plot rectangle after guide measurement."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assigns the current responsive pixel range to the copy."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Uses the copy for marks, ticks, grids, and interaction points."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The source scale is never mutated. This makes one module-level definition safe across container resizes, server rendering, multiple mounted hosts, and facets."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reverse"},{"type":"text","value":" axis option reverses the responsive range without changing the domain:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  reverse: true,\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"categorical-scales-and-bandwidth","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Categorical scales and bandwidth"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the compact band-scale factory for categorical positions:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst categoryScale = () =>\n  scaleBand<string>().paddingInner(0.12).paddingOuter(0.06)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts applies the plot range, reads the scale bandwidth, and treats the mapped value as the center of the band for mark and interaction coordinates. Bars use the primary bandwidth by default."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For grouped bars, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout: group({ scale })"},{"type":"text","value":". The supplied band scale is\ncopied and its range is assigned within the primary band. Grouping is explicit;\nthe default length-channel geometry is stacked."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"color-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Color scales"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Omitting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color.scale"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the chart theme’s ordinal palette for categorical group values:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', z: 'region' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a configured compact ordinal scale for semantic stability:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst regionColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['North', 'South', 'West'],\n  ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', z: 'region' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: regionColor,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Region' }),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The color scale is copied before use. Unlike positional scales, its range is semantic color output and remains the range you configured."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a factory when a custom color mapping should infer its domain:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const color = {\n  scale: () => scaleOrdinal<string, string>().range(['#2563eb', '#f97316']),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upgrade the color mapping to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" when numeric values need sequential or\ndiverging interpolation, or when authored policy needs quantile, quantize, or\nthreshold bins. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale-chromatic"},{"type":"text","value":" supplies optional color schemes; it is a\nseparate direct dependency when imported."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"full-d3-scale-semantics","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full D3 scale semantics"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replace only the compact factory whose contract is insufficient. Import that\nfactory directly from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleUtc"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" preserve elapsed-time spacing and calendar ticks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleLog"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scalePow"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleSymlog"},{"type":"text","value":" express nonlinear quantitative\ncomparisons."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleSqrt"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleRadial"},{"type":"text","value":" map magnitude to symbol radius or area."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleSequential"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleDiverging"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleQuantile"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleQuantize"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleThreshold"},{"type":"text","value":" express quantitative or stepped color policy."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"D3 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleLinear"},{"type":"text","value":" supports piecewise domains and ranges, nonnumeric range\ninterpolation, custom interpolators, and the rest of the complete D3 linear\ncontract."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The factory-versus-instance and responsive-range rules do not change after an\nupgrade."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radius-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Radius scales"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":" treats "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"r"},{"type":"text","value":" as pixels unless "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rScale"},{"type":"text","value":" is supplied:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleSqrt } from 'd3-scale'\n\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  r: 'accounts',\n  rScale: {\n    scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 24]),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The radius factory infers "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[0, maximum]"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"r"},{"type":"text","value":". A configured scale instance\nstill keeps its explicit domain. D3 owns the radius mapping; the chart owns dot\ngeometry and rendering."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"curves","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Curves"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Straight lines and areas do not need "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"},{"type":"text","value":". Opt into a curve only when the design requires it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { curveMonotoneX } from 'd3-shape'\nimport { d3Curve, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  curve: d3Curve(curveMonotoneX),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3Curve"},{"type":"text","value":" adapts a D3 curve factory to the small line-and-area curve contract. Importing it is explicit so a straight chart does not need the shape path."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"areaX"},{"type":"text","value":" marks use the separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3AreaXCurve"},{"type":"text","value":" bridge from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/d3/area-x"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"transforms-produce-rows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transforms produce rows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts includes typed, data-first helpers for common transforms:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { binX } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/bin'\n\nconst histogram = binX(rows, {\n  value: 'value',\n  thresholds: 20,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the result to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barY"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineY"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":", or a custom mark. The helpers\nuse compact row-oriented kernels or isolated granular D3 implementations while\nretaining typed source lineage. Domain-specific D3 transforms still work\ndirectly; no adapter or library-owned series shape is required. Keep\nsubstantial transforms beside the definition and memoize them through\napplication reactivity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same rule applies to stacks, pies, hierarchies, force layouts, and\nserver-prepared intervals: preserve the useful output as typed rows, then map\nit through mark channels. A responsive geographic projection instead belongs\nin "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"},{"type":"text","value":"'s projection factory because its pixel range depends on the final\nplot bounds."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pixel-to-value-inversion","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixel-to-value inversion"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"brushX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"continuousCursor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoomX"},{"type":"text","value":", and free "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursorHost"},{"type":"text","value":" bindings own\nfinal-scale inversion for their normal gestures. A custom crop or gesture can\nread the same optional inverse from the resolved scene scale:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const scene = host.getScene()\nconst invertX = scene.scales.x.invert\nif (!invertX) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible x scale')\n\nconst selectedDate = invertX(pointerX)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a normal continuous y axis, the resolved scale already owns its reversed\npixel range:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const invertY = scene.scales.y.invert\nif (!invertY) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible y scale')\n\nconst selectedValue = invertY(pointerY)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply the application’s precision policy after inversion. For example, round a day-based selection with a D3 time interval or round a currency threshold to the supported increment. Pixels do not imply semantic precision."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A free cursor needs no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valueAt"},{"type":"text","value":" callback for ordinary numeric or temporal\naxes. Provide one only to replace inversion with explicit snapping, rounding,\nor another semantic mapping. Missing and non-invertible scales require that\noverride."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the application owns a custom gesture, disable the native nearest-point\nfocus strategy if the two interactions would conflict. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/interactions-and-selections.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactions and Selections"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"log-scale-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Log-scale example"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"log-scale-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"log-scale","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { scaleLog } from 'd3-scale'\\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { flare, type FlareRow } from './data'\\n\\ntype SizedFlareRow = FlareRow & { size: number }\\n\\nconst rows = flare.filter((row): row is SizedFlareRow => row.size !== null)\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    dot(rows, {\\n      x: 'size',\\n      y: (row) => row.name.split('.').length - 1,\\n      key: 'name',\\n      r: 4,\\n      fill: '#2563eb',\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: scaleLog().domain([200, 30_000]),\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Class size' },\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Hierarchy depth' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export interface FlareRow {\\n  name: string\\n  size: number | null\\n}\\n\\nexport const flare: readonly FlareRow[] = [\\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.cluster', size: 3938 },\\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.graph', size: 10_871 },\\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.optimization', size: 5731 },\\n  { name: 'flare.display', size: 12_867 },\\n  { name: 'flare.query', size: 2779 },\\n  { name: 'flare.unresolved', size: null },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleLog } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { flare, type FlareRow } from './data'\n\ntype SizedFlareRow = FlareRow & { size: number }\n\nconst rows = flare.filter((row): row is SizedFlareRow => row.size !== null)\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'size',\n      y: (row) => row.name.split('.').length - 1,\n      key: 'name',\n      r: 4,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLog().domain([200, 30_000]),\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Class size' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Hierarchy depth' },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=log-scale env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface FlareRow {\n  name: string\n  size: number | null\n}\n\nexport const flare: readonly FlareRow[] = [\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.cluster', size: 3938 },\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.graph', size: 10_871 },\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.optimization', size: 5731 },\n  { name: 'flare.display', size: 12_867 },\n  { name: 'flare.query', size: 2779 },\n  { name: 'flare.unresolved', size: null },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=log-scale file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This chart upgrades only x. Install "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@types/d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" for\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleLog"},{"type":"text","value":"; the ordinary numeric y mapping remains compact."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-scales-are-the-final-extension","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom scales are the final extension"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":" only when neither a compact nor D3 callable scale can express\nthe mapping. Its resolver owns the complete domain, finite mapping, ticks,\nformatting, bandwidth, and response to the supplied chart range. A custom scale\nis appropriate for context-aware mappings that need the resolved chart options,\nnot as a wrapper around an existing compact or D3 scale."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/custom-extensions.md#custom-positional-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Extensions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor the resolver contract."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For chart-side scale, guide, and color types, see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/scales-guides-and-color.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales, Guides, and Color Reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"start-with-compact-scales","text":"Start with compact scales","level":2},{"id":"upgrade-one-scale-at-a-time","text":"Upgrade one scale at a time","level":2},{"id":"direct-dependency-ownership","text":"Direct dependency ownership","level":2},{"id":"capability-map","text":"Capability map","level":2},{"id":"positional-scales-follow-materialized-dimensions","text":"Positional scales follow materialized dimensions","level":2},{"id":"factory-domains-come-from-marks","text":"Factory domains come from marks","level":2},{"id":"fixed-domains-remain-application-semantics","text":"Fixed domains remain application semantics","level":2},{"id":"responsive-ranges-belong-to-tanstack-charts","text":"Responsive ranges belong to TanStack Charts","level":2},{"id":"categorical-scales-and-bandwidth","text":"Categorical scales and bandwidth","level":2},{"id":"color-scales","text":"Color scales","level":2},{"id":"full-d3-scale-semantics","text":"Full D3 scale semantics","level":2},{"id":"radius-scales","text":"Radius scales","level":2},{"id":"curves","text":"Curves","level":2},{"id":"transforms-produce-rows","text":"Transforms produce rows","level":2},{"id":"pixel-to-value-inversion","text":"Pixel-to-value inversion","level":2},{"id":"log-scale-example","text":"Log-scale example","level":2},{"id":"custom-scales-are-the-final-extension","text":"Custom scales are the final extension","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts accepts callable, copyable scale factories and instances. Start\nwith the exact compact scale entry that matches the mapping. Upgrade only the\naxis, color, or radius mapping whose semantics require D3.\n\n- **TanStack compact scales** cover numeric linear, categorical band and point,\n  and ordinal mappings without a production D3 dependency.\n- **D3** adds temporal, nonlinear, radial, interpolated, and statistical scale\n  semantics plus optional shape, time, and spatial algorithms.\n- **TanStack Charts** infers factory domains from mark channels, assigns\n  responsive pixel ranges, lays out guides, compiles scenes, and renders them.\n\nCompact and D3 scales implement the same chart-facing contract and can be used\nin one definition. There is no hidden D3 umbrella import.\n\n`@tanstack/charts` declares `d3-array`, `d3-shape`, `d3-geo`, `d3-delaunay`,\n`d3-hexbin`, `d3-contour`, `d3-force`, `d3-sankey`, and `d3-hierarchy` because its\ntransforms, polar and D3 curve features, geo features, and optional spatial,\nnetwork, and hierarchy entries own those implementations. They are not peers\nand require no `use` configuration. Bundlers tree-shake unused algorithms and\ngeometry, and exact feature subpaths remain available when an application\nwants a narrower import.\n\n## Start with compact scales\n\nInstall TanStack Charts for ordinary numeric and categorical charts:\n\n```sh\npnpm add @tanstack/charts\n```\n\nImport one exact family:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n```\n\nThere is intentionally no aggregate `/scales` export. Each exact scale subpath\nfits the same callable, `domain`, `range`, and `copy` contract consumed by\nTanStack Charts.\n\nThe compact linear scale has numeric, two-stop domains and ranges. It supports\nmapping, `invert`, `clamp`, `nice`, ticks, basic numeric tick formatting, and\ncopying. The categorical families support D3-compatible domain interning,\npadding, alignment, rounding, bandwidth, unknown values, and copying.\n\nChoose the smallest family that preserves the data's meaning:\n\n| Mapping                                                    | Start with                           | Upgrade when                                                                    |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Numeric x or y                                             | `@tanstack/charts/scales/linear`     | The mapping needs piecewise domains, nonnumeric output, or custom interpolation |\n| Categories with width, such as bars                        | `@tanstack/charts/scales/band`       | The mapping needs behavior outside the documented compact band contract         |\n| Categories without width, such as line or dot positions    | `@tanstack/charts/scales/point`      | The values must instead be spaced by elapsed time                               |\n| Stable categorical colors                                  | `@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal`    | The color mapping is sequential, diverging, quantile, quantize, or threshold    |\n| Dates spaced by elapsed time and calendar-aware ticks      | `d3-scale` `scaleTime` or `scaleUtc` | —                                                                               |\n| Logarithmic, power, symlog, square-root, or radial mapping | The corresponding `d3-scale` family  | —                                                                               |\n\n`scaleBand` and `scalePoint` accept `Date` values as categories. They preserve\ndistinct dates and first-seen order, but they do not represent elapsed time. A\nFriday and the following Monday occupy adjacent categorical positions. Use\n`scaleTime` or `scaleUtc` when the weekend must occupy its real temporal span or\nwhen the axis needs calendar-aware ticks.\n\nAn axis formatter does not by itself require a larger scale. Pass\n`Intl.NumberFormat`, `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, or another application formatter to\nthe guide. Upgrade to D3 when the scale's own tick, interpolation, or domain\nsemantics are required.\n\n## Upgrade one scale at a time\n\nA definition does not need one scale implementation for every mapping. This\ntime-series chart upgrades x to D3 while keeping its ordinary numeric y scale\ncompact:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAdd `d3-scale` and `@types/d3-scale` because this source imports `scaleUtc`.\nThe compact scale entries ship their own declarations.\n\n## Direct dependency ownership\n\nIf application source imports a `d3-*` module, declare that module and its\nmatching TypeScript package directly:\n\n```sh\npnpm add d3-scale\npnpm add -D @types/d3-scale\n```\n\nDo not declare a D3 module merely because another package uses it internally. A\nchart that directly upgrades only its temporal axis should declare only\n`d3-scale`; bundlers should not retain unused shape, force, geo, zoom, or\nhierarchy code. Apply the same direct-dependency rule when source imports\n`d3-array`, `d3-shape`, or another granular D3 module.\n\nThis rule also applies when definitions live in framework component source.\nThe adapter mounts a definition; it does not own the D3 imports used to author\nit.\n\n`@tanstack/charts` declares `d3-array`, `d3-shape`, and `d3-geo` because its\nnumeric-bin and stack transforms, polar and D3 curve features, and geo features\nown those implementations. They are not peers and require no `use`\nconfiguration. Bundlers tree-shake unused algorithms and geometry, and exact\nfeature subpaths remain available when an application wants a narrower import.\n\n## Capability map\n\nUse the official D3 pages as the API reference for each algorithm. TanStack Charts documentation only describes how its output crosses the chart boundary.\n\n| Need                                                               | D3 module                                                   | How it enters TanStack Charts                                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Quantitative, temporal, categorical, log, radial, and color scales | [`d3-scale`](https://d3js.org/d3-scale)                     | Pass a factory for an inferred domain or an instance for a fixed domain                                      |\n| Sequential, diverging, and categorical color schemes               | [`d3-scale-chromatic`](https://d3js.org/d3-scale-chromatic) | Pass an interpolator or scheme to a configured D3 color scale                                                |\n| Extents, grouping, aggregation, bins, sorting, and statistics      | [`d3-array`](https://d3js.org/d3-array)                     | Convert source data into rows, domains, or thresholds before creating marks                                  |\n| Stacks, pies, arcs, curves, and shape generators                   | [`d3-shape`](https://d3js.org/d3-shape)                     | Feed pie intervals and curve factories to polar marks, or bridge a Cartesian curve with `d3Curve`            |\n| Calendar intervals                                                 | [`d3-time`](https://d3js.org/d3-time)                       | Build bins, ticks, rounded selections, and date windows in application code                                  |\n| Numeric formatting                                                 | [`d3-format`](https://d3js.org/d3-format)                   | Pass a formatter to an axis or tooltip option                                                                |\n| Time formatting                                                    | [`d3-time-format`](https://d3js.org/d3-time-format)         | Pass a formatter to an axis or tooltip option                                                                |\n| Quadtrees                                                          | [`d3-quadtree`](https://d3js.org/d3-quadtree)               | Implement an optional `ChartSpatialIndexFactory`                                                             |\n| Delaunay and Voronoi geometry                                      | [`d3-delaunay`](https://d3js.org/d3-delaunay)               | Use exact optional `delaunayLink` or `voronoi` marks; import directly for a custom spatial index or geometry |\n| DOM selection for optional D3 gesture controllers                  | [`d3-selection`](https://d3js.org/d3-selection)             | Imported privately by first-party brush and zoom behaviors; import directly for a different DOM controller   |\n| Brushes                                                            | [`d3-brush`](https://d3js.org/d3-brush)                     | Use exact optional `brushX`; import directly for a different application-owned gesture                       |\n| Pan and zoom                                                       | [`d3-zoom`](https://d3js.org/d3-zoom)                       | Use exact optional `zoomX` with a controlled semantic window; import directly for a different gesture policy |\n| Hierarchies and layouts                                            | [`d3-hierarchy`](https://d3js.org/d3-hierarchy)             | Use exact optional `treeLayout` for flat tidy trees and `treemap` for responsive rectangle tiling            |\n| Force simulation                                                   | [`d3-force`](https://d3js.org/d3-force)                     | Use exact optional `forceLayout` for static settlement; import directly for a live application controller    |\n| Sankey flow layout                                                 | [`d3-sankey`](https://github.com/d3/d3-sankey)              | Use exact optional `sankeyDiagram` for responsive layout and ordinary child-mark composition                 |\n| Geographic projections and paths                                   | [`d3-geo`](https://d3js.org/d3-geo)                         | Pass a responsive projection factory to `geoShape`                                                           |\n\nAn optional algorithm does not necessarily need final chart layout.\n`treeLayout` and `forceLayout` produce semantic data-space coordinates, so\nnative `link`, `dot`, and `text` marks can map their output through ordinary\npositional scales. Treemap topology depends on the final plot aspect ratio and\nits padding is measured in pixels, so the exact `treemap` mark owns both the\nresponsive D3 layout and its downward-increasing screen coordinates. Sankey\ncolumn allocation, padding, and proportional link width also resolve in final\npixels; `sankeyDiagram` then composes ordinary marks over immutable node and\nlink rows. Density estimation and Delaunay geometry likewise run after scale\nranges resolve. Keep live force controllers in application state; the exact\nCharts force entry owns deterministic static settlement only.\n\n## Positional scales follow materialized dimensions\n\nEvery materialized positional dimension declares its scale:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst spec = {\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleBand },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nPositionless marks use explicit null entries:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, frame } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst borderOnlyChart = defineChart({\n  marks: [frame()],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nA mark with x values requires a non-null x scale. A mark with y values requires\na non-null y scale. One-dimensional charts use `null` only for the unused\nentry. The scale factory chooses the mapping; materialized mark channels\nsupply its domain.\n\n## Factory domains come from marks\n\nPass the factory itself when the domain should cover the rendered data:\n\n```ts\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'month', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scalePoint },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nContinuous factories use the finite extent of their channels. Band and point\nfactories use distinct values in first-seen order. Bars and areas include zero\nwhen they use an implicit zero baseline. Empty channels retain the factory's\nnative domain.\n\nReturn a scale from a zero-argument factory when it needs configuration before\ndomain inference:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.16),\n}\n```\n\nUse the axis `nice` option because nicening must happen after inference.\n\n## Fixed domains remain application semantics\n\nPass a scale instance when the domain must not follow the rendered marks:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst normalizedY = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]),\n}\n\nconst windowedX = {\n  scale: scaleUtc().domain([windowStart, windowEnd]),\n}\n```\n\nBe equally deliberate with:\n\n- Whether a log scale is valid for all values\n- Whether time is local or UTC\n- Which categories exist when some are filtered out\n- Whether multiple facets share a domain\n- Whether a color domain must remain stable across sessions\n\nThe instance rule is the same for compact and D3 scales. Use the implementation\nthat owns the required mapping semantics, then configure the application-owned\ndomain on that instance.\n\n## Responsive ranges belong to TanStack Charts\n\nDo not assign pixel ranges to positional scales used by the chart:\n\n```ts\nconst xScale = scaleUtc\nconst yScale = scaleLinear\n```\n\nFor each scene, TanStack Charts:\n\n1. Creates a factory scale or copies a configured instance.\n2. Calculates the plot rectangle after guide measurement.\n3. Assigns the current responsive pixel range to the copy.\n4. Uses the copy for marks, ticks, grids, and interaction points.\n\nThe source scale is never mutated. This makes one module-level definition safe across container resizes, server rendering, multiple mounted hosts, and facets.\n\nThe `reverse` axis option reverses the responsive range without changing the domain:\n\n```ts\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  reverse: true,\n}\n```\n\n## Categorical scales and bandwidth\n\nPass the compact band-scale factory for categorical positions:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst categoryScale = () =>\n  scaleBand<string>().paddingInner(0.12).paddingOuter(0.06)\n```\n\nTanStack Charts applies the plot range, reads the scale bandwidth, and treats the mapped value as the center of the band for mark and interaction coordinates. Bars use the primary bandwidth by default.\n\nFor grouped bars, use `layout: group({ scale })`. The supplied band scale is\ncopied and its range is assigned within the primary band. Grouping is explicit;\nthe default length-channel geometry is stacked.\n\n## Color scales\n\nOmitting `color.scale` uses the chart theme’s ordinal palette for categorical group values:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', z: 'region' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nUse a configured compact ordinal scale for semantic stability:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst regionColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['North', 'South', 'West'],\n  ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', z: 'region' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: regionColor,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Region' }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe color scale is copied before use. Unlike positional scales, its range is semantic color output and remains the range you configured.\n\nUse a factory when a custom color mapping should infer its domain:\n\n```ts\nconst color = {\n  scale: () => scaleOrdinal<string, string>().range(['#2563eb', '#f97316']),\n}\n```\n\nUpgrade the color mapping to `d3-scale` when numeric values need sequential or\ndiverging interpolation, or when authored policy needs quantile, quantize, or\nthreshold bins. `d3-scale-chromatic` supplies optional color schemes; it is a\nseparate direct dependency when imported.\n\n## Full D3 scale semantics\n\nReplace only the compact factory whose contract is insufficient. Import that\nfactory directly from `d3-scale`:\n\n- `scaleUtc` and `scaleTime` preserve elapsed-time spacing and calendar ticks.\n- `scaleLog`, `scalePow`, and `scaleSymlog` express nonlinear quantitative\n  comparisons.\n- `scaleSqrt` and `scaleRadial` map magnitude to symbol radius or area.\n- `scaleSequential`, `scaleDiverging`, `scaleQuantile`, `scaleQuantize`, and\n  `scaleThreshold` express quantitative or stepped color policy.\n- D3 `scaleLinear` supports piecewise domains and ranges, nonnumeric range\n  interpolation, custom interpolators, and the rest of the complete D3 linear\n  contract.\n\nThe factory-versus-instance and responsive-range rules do not change after an\nupgrade.\n\n## Radius scales\n\n`dot` treats `r` as pixels unless `rScale` is supplied:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleSqrt } from 'd3-scale'\n\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  r: 'accounts',\n  rScale: {\n    scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 24]),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe radius factory infers `[0, maximum]` from `r`. A configured scale instance\nstill keeps its explicit domain. D3 owns the radius mapping; the chart owns dot\ngeometry and rendering.\n\n## Curves\n\nStraight lines and areas do not need `d3-shape`. Opt into a curve only when the design requires it:\n\n```ts\nimport { curveMonotoneX } from 'd3-shape'\nimport { d3Curve, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  curve: d3Curve(curveMonotoneX),\n})\n```\n\n`d3Curve` adapts a D3 curve factory to the small line-and-area curve contract. Importing it is explicit so a straight chart does not need the shape path.\n\nHorizontal `areaX` marks use the separate `d3AreaXCurve` bridge from `@tanstack/charts/d3/area-x`.\n\n## Transforms produce rows\n\nTanStack Charts includes typed, data-first helpers for common transforms:\n\n```ts\nimport { binX } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/bin'\n\nconst histogram = binX(rows, {\n  value: 'value',\n  thresholds: 20,\n})\n```\n\nPass the result to `rect`, `barY`, `lineY`, `dot`, or a custom mark. The helpers\nuse compact row-oriented kernels or isolated granular D3 implementations while\nretaining typed source lineage. Domain-specific D3 transforms still work\ndirectly; no adapter or library-owned series shape is required. Keep\nsubstantial transforms beside the definition and memoize them through\napplication reactivity.\n\nThe same rule applies to stacks, pies, hierarchies, force layouts, and\nserver-prepared intervals: preserve the useful output as typed rows, then map\nit through mark channels. A responsive geographic projection instead belongs\nin `geoShape`'s projection factory because its pixel range depends on the final\nplot bounds.\n\n## Pixel-to-value inversion\n\n`brushX`, `continuousCursor`, `zoomX`, and free `cursorHost` bindings own\nfinal-scale inversion for their normal gestures. A custom crop or gesture can\nread the same optional inverse from the resolved scene scale:\n\n```ts\nconst scene = host.getScene()\nconst invertX = scene.scales.x.invert\nif (!invertX) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible x scale')\n\nconst selectedDate = invertX(pointerX)\n```\n\nFor a normal continuous y axis, the resolved scale already owns its reversed\npixel range:\n\n```ts\nconst invertY = scene.scales.y.invert\nif (!invertY) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible y scale')\n\nconst selectedValue = invertY(pointerY)\n```\n\nApply the application’s precision policy after inversion. For example, round a day-based selection with a D3 time interval or round a currency threshold to the supported increment. Pixels do not imply semantic precision.\n\nA free cursor needs no `valueAt` callback for ordinary numeric or temporal\naxes. Provide one only to replace inversion with explicit snapping, rounding,\nor another semantic mapping. Missing and non-invertible scales require that\noverride.\n\nWhen the application owns a custom gesture, disable the native nearest-point\nfocus strategy if the two interactions would conflict. See\n[Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md).\n\n## Log-scale example\n\n```ts group=log-scale env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleLog } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { flare, type FlareRow } from './data'\n\ntype SizedFlareRow = FlareRow & { size: number }\n\nconst rows = flare.filter((row): row is SizedFlareRow => row.size !== null)\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'size',\n      y: (row) => row.name.split('.').length - 1,\n      key: 'name',\n      r: 4,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLog().domain([200, 30_000]),\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Class size' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Hierarchy depth' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=log-scale file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface FlareRow {\n  name: string\n  size: number | null\n}\n\nexport const flare: readonly FlareRow[] = [\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.cluster', size: 3938 },\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.graph', size: 10_871 },\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.optimization', size: 5731 },\n  { name: 'flare.display', size: 12_867 },\n  { name: 'flare.query', size: 2779 },\n  { name: 'flare.unresolved', size: null },\n]\n```\n\nThis chart upgrades only x. Install `d3-scale` and `@types/d3-scale` for\n`scaleLog`; the ordinary numeric y mapping remains compact.\n\n## Custom scales are the final extension\n\nUse `ChartScale` only when neither a compact nor D3 callable scale can express\nthe mapping. Its resolver owns the complete domain, finite mapping, ticks,\nformatting, bandwidth, and response to the supplied chart range. A custom scale\nis appropriate for context-aware mappings that need the resolved chart options,\nnot as a wrapper around an existing compact or D3 scale.\n\nSee\n[Custom Extensions](../reference/custom-extensions.md#custom-positional-scales)\nfor the resolver contract.\n\nFor chart-side scale, guide, and color types, see\n[Scales, Guides, and Color Reference](../reference/scales-guides-and-color.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Scales\ndescription: Start with compact scales, upgrade individual mappings to D3 when their semantics require it, and keep responsive range ownership clear.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts accepts callable, copyable scale factories and instances. Start\nwith the exact compact scale entry that matches the mapping. Upgrade only the\naxis, color, or radius mapping whose semantics require D3.\n\n- **TanStack compact scales** cover numeric linear, categorical band and point,\n  and ordinal mappings without a production D3 dependency.\n- **D3** adds temporal, nonlinear, radial, interpolated, and statistical scale\n  semantics plus optional shape, time, and spatial algorithms.\n- **TanStack Charts** infers factory domains from mark channels, assigns\n  responsive pixel ranges, lays out guides, compiles scenes, and renders them.\n\nCompact and D3 scales implement the same chart-facing contract and can be used\nin one definition. There is no hidden D3 umbrella import.\n\n`@tanstack/charts` declares `d3-array`, `d3-shape`, `d3-geo`, `d3-delaunay`,\n`d3-hexbin`, `d3-contour`, `d3-force`, `d3-sankey`, and `d3-hierarchy` because its\ntransforms, polar and D3 curve features, geo features, and optional spatial,\nnetwork, and hierarchy entries own those implementations. They are not peers\nand require no `use` configuration. Bundlers tree-shake unused algorithms and\ngeometry, and exact feature subpaths remain available when an application\nwants a narrower import.\n\n## Start with compact scales\n\nInstall TanStack Charts for ordinary numeric and categorical charts:\n\n```sh\npnpm add @tanstack/charts\n```\n\nImport one exact family:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n```\n\nThere is intentionally no aggregate `/scales` export. Each exact scale subpath\nfits the same callable, `domain`, `range`, and `copy` contract consumed by\nTanStack Charts.\n\nThe compact linear scale has numeric, two-stop domains and ranges. It supports\nmapping, `invert`, `clamp`, `nice`, ticks, basic numeric tick formatting, and\ncopying. The categorical families support D3-compatible domain interning,\npadding, alignment, rounding, bandwidth, unknown values, and copying.\n\nChoose the smallest family that preserves the data's meaning:\n\n| Mapping                                                    | Start with                           | Upgrade when                                                                    |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Numeric x or y                                             | `@tanstack/charts/scales/linear`     | The mapping needs piecewise domains, nonnumeric output, or custom interpolation |\n| Categories with width, such as bars                        | `@tanstack/charts/scales/band`       | The mapping needs behavior outside the documented compact band contract         |\n| Categories without width, such as line or dot positions    | `@tanstack/charts/scales/point`      | The values must instead be spaced by elapsed time                               |\n| Stable categorical colors                                  | `@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal`    | The color mapping is sequential, diverging, quantile, quantize, or threshold    |\n| Dates spaced by elapsed time and calendar-aware ticks      | `d3-scale` `scaleTime` or `scaleUtc` | —                                                                               |\n| Logarithmic, power, symlog, square-root, or radial mapping | The corresponding `d3-scale` family  | —                                                                               |\n\n`scaleBand` and `scalePoint` accept `Date` values as categories. They preserve\ndistinct dates and first-seen order, but they do not represent elapsed time. A\nFriday and the following Monday occupy adjacent categorical positions. Use\n`scaleTime` or `scaleUtc` when the weekend must occupy its real temporal span or\nwhen the axis needs calendar-aware ticks.\n\nAn axis formatter does not by itself require a larger scale. Pass\n`Intl.NumberFormat`, `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, or another application formatter to\nthe guide. Upgrade to D3 when the scale's own tick, interpolation, or domain\nsemantics are required.\n\n## Upgrade one scale at a time\n\nA definition does not need one scale implementation for every mapping. This\ntime-series chart upgrades x to D3 while keeping its ordinary numeric y scale\ncompact:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAdd `d3-scale` and `@types/d3-scale` because this source imports `scaleUtc`.\nThe compact scale entries ship their own declarations.\n\n## Direct dependency ownership\n\nIf application source imports a `d3-*` module, declare that module and its\nmatching TypeScript package directly:\n\n```sh\npnpm add d3-scale\npnpm add -D @types/d3-scale\n```\n\nDo not declare a D3 module merely because another package uses it internally. A\nchart that directly upgrades only its temporal axis should declare only\n`d3-scale`; bundlers should not retain unused shape, force, geo, zoom, or\nhierarchy code. Apply the same direct-dependency rule when source imports\n`d3-array`, `d3-shape`, or another granular D3 module.\n\nThis rule also applies when definitions live in framework component source.\nThe adapter mounts a definition; it does not own the D3 imports used to author\nit.\n\n`@tanstack/charts` declares `d3-array`, `d3-shape`, and `d3-geo` because its\nnumeric-bin and stack transforms, polar and D3 curve features, and geo features\nown those implementations. They are not peers and require no `use`\nconfiguration. Bundlers tree-shake unused algorithms and geometry, and exact\nfeature subpaths remain available when an application wants a narrower import.\n\n## Capability map\n\nUse the official D3 pages as the API reference for each algorithm. TanStack Charts documentation only describes how its output crosses the chart boundary.\n\n| Need                                                               | D3 module                                                   | How it enters TanStack Charts                                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Quantitative, temporal, categorical, log, radial, and color scales | [`d3-scale`](https://d3js.org/d3-scale)                     | Pass a factory for an inferred domain or an instance for a fixed domain                                      |\n| Sequential, diverging, and categorical color schemes               | [`d3-scale-chromatic`](https://d3js.org/d3-scale-chromatic) | Pass an interpolator or scheme to a configured D3 color scale                                                |\n| Extents, grouping, aggregation, bins, sorting, and statistics      | [`d3-array`](https://d3js.org/d3-array)                     | Convert source data into rows, domains, or thresholds before creating marks                                  |\n| Stacks, pies, arcs, curves, and shape generators                   | [`d3-shape`](https://d3js.org/d3-shape)                     | Feed pie intervals and curve factories to polar marks, or bridge a Cartesian curve with `d3Curve`            |\n| Calendar intervals                                                 | [`d3-time`](https://d3js.org/d3-time)                       | Build bins, ticks, rounded selections, and date windows in application code                                  |\n| Numeric formatting                                                 | [`d3-format`](https://d3js.org/d3-format)                   | Pass a formatter to an axis or tooltip option                                                                |\n| Time formatting                                                    | [`d3-time-format`](https://d3js.org/d3-time-format)         | Pass a formatter to an axis or tooltip option                                                                |\n| Quadtrees                                                          | [`d3-quadtree`](https://d3js.org/d3-quadtree)               | Implement an optional `ChartSpatialIndexFactory`                                                             |\n| Delaunay and Voronoi geometry                                      | [`d3-delaunay`](https://d3js.org/d3-delaunay)               | Use exact optional `delaunayLink` or `voronoi` marks; import directly for a custom spatial index or geometry |\n| DOM selection for optional D3 gesture controllers                  | [`d3-selection`](https://d3js.org/d3-selection)             | Imported privately by first-party brush and zoom behaviors; import directly for a different DOM controller   |\n| Brushes                                                            | [`d3-brush`](https://d3js.org/d3-brush)                     | Use exact optional `brushX`; import directly for a different application-owned gesture                       |\n| Pan and zoom                                                       | [`d3-zoom`](https://d3js.org/d3-zoom)                       | Use exact optional `zoomX` with a controlled semantic window; import directly for a different gesture policy |\n| Hierarchies and layouts                                            | [`d3-hierarchy`](https://d3js.org/d3-hierarchy)             | Use exact optional `treeLayout` for flat tidy trees and `treemap` for responsive rectangle tiling            |\n| Force simulation                                                   | [`d3-force`](https://d3js.org/d3-force)                     | Use exact optional `forceLayout` for static settlement; import directly for a live application controller    |\n| Sankey flow layout                                                 | [`d3-sankey`](https://github.com/d3/d3-sankey)              | Use exact optional `sankeyDiagram` for responsive layout and ordinary child-mark composition                 |\n| Geographic projections and paths                                   | [`d3-geo`](https://d3js.org/d3-geo)                         | Pass a responsive projection factory to `geoShape`                                                           |\n\nAn optional algorithm does not necessarily need final chart layout.\n`treeLayout` and `forceLayout` produce semantic data-space coordinates, so\nnative `link`, `dot`, and `text` marks can map their output through ordinary\npositional scales. Treemap topology depends on the final plot aspect ratio and\nits padding is measured in pixels, so the exact `treemap` mark owns both the\nresponsive D3 layout and its downward-increasing screen coordinates. Sankey\ncolumn allocation, padding, and proportional link width also resolve in final\npixels; `sankeyDiagram` then composes ordinary marks over immutable node and\nlink rows. Density estimation and Delaunay geometry likewise run after scale\nranges resolve. Keep live force controllers in application state; the exact\nCharts force entry owns deterministic static settlement only.\n\n## Positional scales follow materialized dimensions\n\nEvery materialized positional dimension declares its scale:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst spec = {\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleBand },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nPositionless marks use explicit null entries:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, frame } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst borderOnlyChart = defineChart({\n  marks: [frame()],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nA mark with x values requires a non-null x scale. A mark with y values requires\na non-null y scale. One-dimensional charts use `null` only for the unused\nentry. The scale factory chooses the mapping; materialized mark channels\nsupply its domain.\n\n## Factory domains come from marks\n\nPass the factory itself when the domain should cover the rendered data:\n\n```ts\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'month', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scalePoint },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nContinuous factories use the finite extent of their channels. Band and point\nfactories use distinct values in first-seen order. Bars and areas include zero\nwhen they use an implicit zero baseline. Empty channels retain the factory's\nnative domain.\n\nReturn a scale from a zero-argument factory when it needs configuration before\ndomain inference:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst x = {\n  scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.16),\n}\n```\n\nUse the axis `nice` option because nicening must happen after inference.\n\n## Fixed domains remain application semantics\n\nPass a scale instance when the domain must not follow the rendered marks:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst normalizedY = {\n  scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]),\n}\n\nconst windowedX = {\n  scale: scaleUtc().domain([windowStart, windowEnd]),\n}\n```\n\nBe equally deliberate with:\n\n- Whether a log scale is valid for all values\n- Whether time is local or UTC\n- Which categories exist when some are filtered out\n- Whether multiple facets share a domain\n- Whether a color domain must remain stable across sessions\n\nThe instance rule is the same for compact and D3 scales. Use the implementation\nthat owns the required mapping semantics, then configure the application-owned\ndomain on that instance.\n\n## Responsive ranges belong to TanStack Charts\n\nDo not assign pixel ranges to positional scales used by the chart:\n\n```ts\nconst xScale = scaleUtc\nconst yScale = scaleLinear\n```\n\nFor each scene, TanStack Charts:\n\n1. Creates a factory scale or copies a configured instance.\n2. Calculates the plot rectangle after guide measurement.\n3. Assigns the current responsive pixel range to the copy.\n4. Uses the copy for marks, ticks, grids, and interaction points.\n\nThe source scale is never mutated. This makes one module-level definition safe across container resizes, server rendering, multiple mounted hosts, and facets.\n\nThe `reverse` axis option reverses the responsive range without changing the domain:\n\n```ts\nconst y = {\n  scale: scaleLinear,\n  reverse: true,\n}\n```\n\n## Categorical scales and bandwidth\n\nPass the compact band-scale factory for categorical positions:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\n\nconst categoryScale = () =>\n  scaleBand<string>().paddingInner(0.12).paddingOuter(0.06)\n```\n\nTanStack Charts applies the plot range, reads the scale bandwidth, and treats the mapped value as the center of the band for mark and interaction coordinates. Bars use the primary bandwidth by default.\n\nFor grouped bars, use `layout: group({ scale })`. The supplied band scale is\ncopied and its range is assigned within the primary band. Grouping is explicit;\nthe default length-channel geometry is stacked.\n\n## Color scales\n\nOmitting `color.scale` uses the chart theme’s ordinal palette for categorical group values:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', z: 'region' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nUse a configured compact ordinal scale for semantic stability:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst regionColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['North', 'South', 'West'],\n  ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', z: 'region' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: regionColor,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Region' }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe color scale is copied before use. Unlike positional scales, its range is semantic color output and remains the range you configured.\n\nUse a factory when a custom color mapping should infer its domain:\n\n```ts\nconst color = {\n  scale: () => scaleOrdinal<string, string>().range(['#2563eb', '#f97316']),\n}\n```\n\nUpgrade the color mapping to `d3-scale` when numeric values need sequential or\ndiverging interpolation, or when authored policy needs quantile, quantize, or\nthreshold bins. `d3-scale-chromatic` supplies optional color schemes; it is a\nseparate direct dependency when imported.\n\n## Full D3 scale semantics\n\nReplace only the compact factory whose contract is insufficient. Import that\nfactory directly from `d3-scale`:\n\n- `scaleUtc` and `scaleTime` preserve elapsed-time spacing and calendar ticks.\n- `scaleLog`, `scalePow`, and `scaleSymlog` express nonlinear quantitative\n  comparisons.\n- `scaleSqrt` and `scaleRadial` map magnitude to symbol radius or area.\n- `scaleSequential`, `scaleDiverging`, `scaleQuantile`, `scaleQuantize`, and\n  `scaleThreshold` express quantitative or stepped color policy.\n- D3 `scaleLinear` supports piecewise domains and ranges, nonnumeric range\n  interpolation, custom interpolators, and the rest of the complete D3 linear\n  contract.\n\nThe factory-versus-instance and responsive-range rules do not change after an\nupgrade.\n\n## Radius scales\n\n`dot` treats `r` as pixels unless `rScale` is supplied:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleSqrt } from 'd3-scale'\n\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  r: 'accounts',\n  rScale: {\n    scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 24]),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe radius factory infers `[0, maximum]` from `r`. A configured scale instance\nstill keeps its explicit domain. D3 owns the radius mapping; the chart owns dot\ngeometry and rendering.\n\n## Curves\n\nStraight lines and areas do not need `d3-shape`. Opt into a curve only when the design requires it:\n\n```ts\nimport { curveMonotoneX } from 'd3-shape'\nimport { d3Curve, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  curve: d3Curve(curveMonotoneX),\n})\n```\n\n`d3Curve` adapts a D3 curve factory to the small line-and-area curve contract. Importing it is explicit so a straight chart does not need the shape path.\n\nHorizontal `areaX` marks use the separate `d3AreaXCurve` bridge from `@tanstack/charts/d3/area-x`.\n\n## Transforms produce rows\n\nTanStack Charts includes typed, data-first helpers for common transforms:\n\n```ts\nimport { binX } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/bin'\n\nconst histogram = binX(rows, {\n  value: 'value',\n  thresholds: 20,\n})\n```\n\nPass the result to `rect`, `barY`, `lineY`, `dot`, or a custom mark. The helpers\nuse compact row-oriented kernels or isolated granular D3 implementations while\nretaining typed source lineage. Domain-specific D3 transforms still work\ndirectly; no adapter or library-owned series shape is required. Keep\nsubstantial transforms beside the definition and memoize them through\napplication reactivity.\n\nThe same rule applies to stacks, pies, hierarchies, force layouts, and\nserver-prepared intervals: preserve the useful output as typed rows, then map\nit through mark channels. A responsive geographic projection instead belongs\nin `geoShape`'s projection factory because its pixel range depends on the final\nplot bounds.\n\n## Pixel-to-value inversion\n\n`brushX`, `continuousCursor`, `zoomX`, and free `cursorHost` bindings own\nfinal-scale inversion for their normal gestures. A custom crop or gesture can\nread the same optional inverse from the resolved scene scale:\n\n```ts\nconst scene = host.getScene()\nconst invertX = scene.scales.x.invert\nif (!invertX) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible x scale')\n\nconst selectedDate = invertX(pointerX)\n```\n\nFor a normal continuous y axis, the resolved scale already owns its reversed\npixel range:\n\n```ts\nconst invertY = scene.scales.y.invert\nif (!invertY) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible y scale')\n\nconst selectedValue = invertY(pointerY)\n```\n\nApply the application’s precision policy after inversion. For example, round a day-based selection with a D3 time interval or round a currency threshold to the supported increment. Pixels do not imply semantic precision.\n\nA free cursor needs no `valueAt` callback for ordinary numeric or temporal\naxes. Provide one only to replace inversion with explicit snapping, rounding,\nor another semantic mapping. Missing and non-invertible scales require that\noverride.\n\nWhen the application owns a custom gesture, disable the native nearest-point\nfocus strategy if the two interactions would conflict. See\n[Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md).\n\n## Log-scale example\n\n```ts group=log-scale env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleLog } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { flare, type FlareRow } from './data'\n\ntype SizedFlareRow = FlareRow & { size: number }\n\nconst rows = flare.filter((row): row is SizedFlareRow => row.size !== null)\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'size',\n      y: (row) => row.name.split('.').length - 1,\n      key: 'name',\n      r: 4,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLog().domain([200, 30_000]),\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Class size' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Hierarchy depth' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=log-scale file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface FlareRow {\n  name: string\n  size: number | null\n}\n\nexport const flare: readonly FlareRow[] = [\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.cluster', size: 3938 },\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.graph', size: 10_871 },\n  { name: 'flare.analytics.optimization', size: 5731 },\n  { name: 'flare.display', size: 12_867 },\n  { name: 'flare.query', size: 2779 },\n  { name: 'flare.unresolved', size: null },\n]\n```\n\nThis chart upgrades only x. Install `d3-scale` and `@types/d3-scale` for\n`scaleLog`; the ordinary numeric y mapping remains compact.\n\n## Custom scales are the final extension\n\nUse `ChartScale` only when neither a compact nor D3 callable scale can express\nthe mapping. Its resolver owns the complete domain, finite mapping, ticks,\nformatting, bandwidth, and response to the supplied chart range. A custom scale\nis appropriate for context-aware mappings that need the resolved chart options,\nnot as a wrapper around an existing compact or D3 scale.\n\nSee\n[Custom Extensions](../reference/custom-extensions.md#custom-positional-scales)\nfor the resolver contract.\n\nFor chart-side scale, guide, and color types, see\n[Scales, Guides, and Color Reference](../reference/scales-guides-and-color.md).\n"}
